WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:04.330
In 2012, Superstorm Sandy pummeled the east
coast of America.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:10.820
Winds blew in at 80 miles per hour and heavy
rain pushed the ocean over 9 feet above average levels.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:16.900
In New York, storm surges brought 30-foot
waves ashore, flooding streets, subways, and homes.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.940
20 miles south of Manhattan – several neighborhoods
on the small borough of Staten Island sat in ruins.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.480
Homes were completely leveled and 24 people
were killed.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.660
As global temperatures rise, research has
shown that increasingly violent storms will

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.370
likely continue assaulting our coasts.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:38.050
Paired with rising sea levels, that could
mean higher risk of flooding and worsening

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.050
catastrophic damage

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.630
to areas like this – unless we learn to
adapt.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.680
And for Staten Island, that means building
a wall.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:54.480
Adaptation is this idea that we are physically
remaking our cities to make sure that they

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:57.920
are going to be resilient in the climate disasters
of the future.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.140
And that will also include retrofitting our cities for the everyday effects of climate change,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.380
which means things like extreme heat, heavy downpours, drought.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.060
A sea wall is one of these adaptations.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.940
It’s erected parallel to the coast to create
a “hard shoreline”.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:17.760
It deflects daily tides to keep the shore
from eroding away as it would under normal conditions.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.290
If a natural disaster strikes, it’s meant
to stop incoming storm surges or a tsunami.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:29.690
Now, if fortifying our cities with walls seems
too simple and medieval, that’s because

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.160
it kind of is.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.760
Climate change isn’t a single assault on
a castle, it’s an ongoing, worsening crisis.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.600
And in order to evolve with that crisis, the
adaptations we make need to include resilient infrastructure.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.420
Resiliency is this idea that your city can
respond to any type of threat – it might

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:50.620
not just be a climate threat, but that you
plan for disasters and emergencies in a way

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.770
that will enrich everyday life for your residents
anyway.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:57.729
In other words, building resilient infrastructure
means we don’t have to choose between feeling

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:02.890
safe and enjoying our cities.
For example, in 2008 New York Harbor School

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:08.020
started the Billion Oyster Project, a resiliency
plan that restores oyster reefs in New York Harbor.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.180
The reefs help filter the water and slow down
powerful waves,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.240
preventing erosion and reducing flooding.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:17.840
The project is even doubling as an educational
program for students around the city.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.760
When natural solutions like that aren’t
enough, cities may look for more intense options.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.519
Like sea walls.

00:02:23.519 --> 00:02:29.599
In hopes to prevent another Sandy-level disaster,
Staten Island has recently received $615 million

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:31.650
in funding for its own sea wall.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.689
The structure will span about 5 miles and
sit 20 feet above sea level.

00:02:35.689 --> 00:02:39.499
And it includes resilient design elements:
The wall will double as a boardwalk, making

00:02:39.499 --> 00:02:42.909
it far less imposing and allowing residents
to enjoy the coastal views.

00:02:42.909 --> 00:02:47.489
Plus, to keep Staten Island safe, the plan
includes adaptations to the land surrounding

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:49.060
the seawall.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:55.279
Globally, rainfall during major storms is
expected to increase 20% by 2100.

00:02:55.279 --> 00:02:59.870
Since much of Staten Island was initially built on
wetlands, intense rainfall could easily flood

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.749
neighborhoods — something a sea wall can’t
prevent.

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:06.569
So the resiliency plan includes returning
several areas on the island to their natural

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:07.569
marshy state.

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:11.180
And that helps to mitigate that storm surge
when you have that coastal flooding, when

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.840
you have heavy rain, and make sure that the
water is not going to be trapped in where

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.109
the buildings are.

00:03:16.109 --> 00:03:19.620
Several man-made ponds will also be excavated
for drainage purposes.

00:03:20.389 --> 00:03:23.859
These alterations required careful planning
and analysis of the land they’re trying

00:03:23.859 --> 00:03:24.859
to defend.

00:03:24.859 --> 00:03:28.359
And altogether, they make a pretty well-rounded
protection plan.

00:03:28.359 --> 00:03:34.060
But even with a multifaceted and resilient
approach, these adaptations might not be enough.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:41.280
In 2018, New Orleans finished an upgrade on
seawalls and levees designed to protect the city.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.659
But 14 billion dollars later, engineers are
saying the walls will be inadequate in 4 years

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.000
due to sea level rise and sinking land.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.320
And in Kerala, India, sea walls have actually
led to worsening coastal erosion.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.260
Climate change can be unpredictable, and we’re
being put in a position to predict it anyway.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:04.720
Resilient infrastructure is one way to tackle
that uncertainty, but it’s not foolproof.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:10.219
Any type of infrastructure we are building
to save ourselves from the adverse impacts

00:04:10.219 --> 00:04:15.540
of climate change is also going to introduce
changes to our city that we are going to have

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.650
to learn to live with.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:21.900
It's moving beyond just building this one
piece of infrastructure that would stay there

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.270
for a century or two and really figuring out
how you can innovate on the materials and

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:31.290
the design over time, to address different
challenges that will surface.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:35.590
The reality is, that even with resiliency
in mind, walling off the ocean is a band-aid

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:37.460
solution on a growing wound.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:40.520
Building a wall to protect our cities is one
thing –

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.880
Building our cities to protect themselves
is another.

